ement and reduced toughness. [Data: Entities (101); Entities (728)]\\n\\nThe data also distinguish bainite from acicular ferrite and note that mechanical behavior and deformation response depend on the surrounding microstructure and processing history. [Data: Entities (101)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Processing and hardenability are linked to bainitic structure\",\"explanation\":\"Steel with medium hardenability can become bainitic in the conventionally hot-rolled or normalized condition. This establishes a direct relationship between hardenability, processing condition, and the formation of bainite. [Data: Entities (892); Relationships (1200)]\\n\\nThe data additionally state that conventional hot rolling of coarse austenite produced bainite together with coarse ferrite. This coarse bainitic-ferritic structure can deteriorate notch toughness, making the processing route and prior austenite condition relevant to material performance. [Data: Relationships (103); Entities (101)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Pearlite content and banding affect steel performance\",\"explanation\":\"Pearlite commonly occurs alongside ferrite in ferrite–pearlite structures, including controlled-rolled steels. The amount and distribution of pearlite are important because pearlite bands can provide paths along which separation cracks propagate. [Data: Entities (102)]\\n\\nLow-pearlite steels reduce the pearlite content, while pearlite-reduced and pearlite-free steels minimize or eliminate it. These modifications are intended to improve weldability and toughness while retaining suitable strength and controlled-rolling performance. [Data: Entities (102); Relationships (1689)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Multiple microstructural routes can support separation cracking\",\"explanation\":\"The community’s central crack-propagation entity states that separation crack propagation can follow grains with {100} orientation, bainite, and/or a pearlite band, depending on the situation. This indicates that separation cracking is ass